items.question:
this debounce fires on every keystroke instead of once, fix it:

function debounce(fn, wait) {
  let timer;
  return function (...args) {
    clearTimeout(timer);
    setTimeout(() => fn.apply(this, args), wait);
  };
}

items.answer:
The bug is that the timer is never stored, so `clearTimeout(timer)` always clears `undefined` and every keystroke schedules its own call. Assign the result:

```javascript
function debounce(fn, wait) {
  let timer;
  return function (...args) {
    clearTimeout(timer);
    timer = setTimeout(() => fn.apply(this, args), wait);
  };
}
```

Now each keystroke cancels the previous pending call, so `fn` runs once after typing stops.

```python
def is_palindrome(s: str) -> bool:
    left, right = 0, len(s) - 1
    while left < right:
        while left < right and not s[left].isalnum():
            left += 1
        while left < right and not s[right].isalnum():
            right -= 1
        if s[left].lower() != s[right].lower():
            return False
        left += 1
        right -= 1
    return True
```

Two pointers start at each end and walk inward, skipping anything that isn't alphanumeric and comparing the rest lowercased, so it never builds a second string. Time is O(n) and space is O(1).

The bug is that `Arrays.sort` on a `char[]` sorts in place and returns `void`, so `Arrays.sort(a).equals(...)` wouldn't even compile. If it's written as two separate sort calls and then `a.equals(b)`, that's the real problem: arrays don't override `equals`, so it falls back to reference identity and is always false for two different arrays.

```java
boolean isAnagram(String s, String t) {
    if (s.length() != t.length()) return false;
    char[] a = s.toCharArray();
    char[] b = t.toCharArray();
    Arrays.sort(a);
    Arrays.sort(b);
    return Arrays.equals(a, b);
}
```

`Arrays.equals` compares element by element, so sorted `listen` and `silent` both become `eilnst` and match. That's O(n log n) time, and the length check short-circuits the obvious mismatch early.

